    "https://careers.umass.edu/jobs/assistant-professor-construction-management-advanced-building-technology-amherst-massachusetts-united-states": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Construction Management / Advanced Building Technology",
      "description": "Assistant Professor - Construction Management / Advanced Building Technology - Amherst, Massachusetts, United States Assistant Professor - Construction Management / Advanced Building Technology - Amherst, Massachusetts, United States Kiosk mode Assistant Professor - Construction Management / Advanced Building Technology 509915 Amherst, Massachusetts, United States Academic Year (9 Months) College of Natural Sciences Full Time MSP On Site Tenure Track Show More Show Less Faculty Opening on: Sep 28 2026 Add to favorites View favorites Title: Assistant Professor - Construction Management / Advanced Building Technology Executive Area: Academic Affairs College/School/MBU: College of Natural Sciences Department: Environmental Conservation Work Location: Amherst Schedule: Full Time Work Arrangement: Onsite Job Summary The Building and Construction Technology (BCT) program in the Department of Environmental Conservation (ECO) at UMass Amherst ( https://www.umass.edu/bct/) seeks applications for a full-time (9-month academic-year) tenure-track appointment as Assistant Professor in Construction Management with an appointment breakdown of 40% research, 40% teaching, and 20% service. We are seeking talented applicants qualified for an assistant professor position. Under exceptional circumstances, highly qualified candidates at other ranks may receive consideration. The earliest start date is September 1, 2027. The position emphasizes rigorous research approaches and active learning strategies. Candidates must care deeply about educating about the sustainable built environment and be a teacher, mentor, and scientist whose broad interests support pedagogies that help students analyze and solve complex problems in the built environment and in a global context. Essential Functions Candidates with backgrounds and research experience in any of the following (or related) areas are encouraged to apply: Construction automation and robotics Advanced digital construction management systems BIM and data-driven construction Digital twins for the built environment Drones, photogrammetry, VR/AR Integrated building design and construction execution Construction risk assessment Construction project controls and economics Construction safety management Construction cost and estimating Lean construction AI and machine learning in construction These topics supplement our current research and teaching directions that center on building materials and structures, building science and energy, and construction processes. Across these areas, candidates may also demonstrate how construction management and digital tools can support sustainable and high-performance project delivery, including applications such as material tracking, construction verification, performance monitoring, and data-informed decision-making. As a result, the applicant will have many opportunities to collaborate with other BCT faculty, as well as faculty from engineering, architecture, business, and other groups like UMass Air (for drones) or the VR/AR group. The successful candidate will teach at least two three-credit courses per year, including relevant courses such as: Construction project control Construction cost management Construction risk management Construction automation and robotics Construction heavy equipment Construction safety management Preconstruction management Lean construction Artificial intelligence applications in construction VDC/BIM for construction management Plans and specifications Service responsibilities include student mentoring and supervision; maintaining student academic record files; interacting with the Registrar’s Office and the honors program to manage degree requirements; and related activities, as well as student recruiting. Work outside of regular hours (2-3 times per semester) may be required to assist with recruitment and other student-focused activities. Other Functions Work collaboratively and effectively to promote teamwork, equality and inclusiveness. Work in partnership with colleagues within the CNS community and across the campus to support the Dean’s strategic priorities. Perform other duties as assigned in support of the mission and goals of the College of Natural Sciences. Minimum Qualifications Candidates must hold a Ph.D. in Construction Management, Construction Engineering, Civil Engineering, Industrial Engineering (applied to Construction Management), or a closely related discipline by the appointment start date. Proficiency in information technology and construction software is essential, as well as a passion for environmental stewardship in the built environment. Candidates should have relevant research experience and a publication record. Although a proven ability to secure funding is preferred, it is not required. Candidates must be willing to pursue external funding and collaborative proposals with agencies such as NSF, DOTs, DoE, NIST, state agencies, and industry consortia, and to assemble multidisciplinary research teams. A candidate should demonstrate strong oral and written communication skills, preferably with university teaching experience, along with an aptitude for interdisciplinary problem solving. Strong applicants will demonstrate depth in one or more areas, an ability to integrate emerging digital approaches with core Construction Management workflows, and a clear trajectory for future research and teaching. This may be reflected through how candidates connect these strengths to project controls, coordination, quality, performance, and other construction-management decisions in both their teaching and research. The successful candidate will be creative and demonstrate a team-player attitude. They must have an understanding and interest in the sensitive integration of built and natural environments. In addition to serving Building and Construction Technology, the candidate will also interface with the departments of Architecture, and Landscape Architecture & Regional Planning, and the College of Engineering, so an interdisciplinary mindset is important. The successful candidate will serve, as do all other faculty, as a mentor to minorities and other underrepresented groups within the Department. UMass Amherst places special emphasis on faculty-student interaction and a commitment to teach and attract a diverse student body. Additional Details Building and Construction Technology (BCT) is one of the programs in the Department of Environmental Conservation (ECO) within the College of Natural Sciences (CNS) and the School of Earth and Sustainability at UMass Amherst. We are co-located with the Departments of Architecture, and Landscape Architecture & Regional Planning in the UMass Olver Design Building, a nationally acclaimed sustainable mass-timber building on the Amherst campus. BCT provides undergraduate and graduate education in construction management, sustainable building systems, advanced building products, and building science. Our program conducts research in these fields and has physical labs for wood mechanics, building science, and construction technology. We also host the world’s first Trimble Technology Lab. The ECO department hosts a multi-disciplinary group of faculty with nationally ranked programs in Building and Construction Technology, Natural Resources Conservation, Environmental Science, and Sustainability Science. Our department has an established culture of collegiality and collaboration between faculty, students, staff, industry, and industry/government partners. We commonly teach interdisciplinary classes and share a fundamental commitment to serving a diverse student body and the broader public. UMass Amherst is part of the Five College Consortium in the beautiful Pioneer Valley of Western Massachusetts, with excellent social, cultural, and recreational amenities in a town and rural setting. We are 2 hours from Boston, 3 hours from New York City, and 4.5 hours from Montreal. At UMass Amherst and in CNS, we strive to be a community where every individual feels a sense of belonging, where every individual is included, and where every individual is valued. These values and goals are reflected in the College of Natural Sciences’ Inclusive Excellence framework and in the UMass Amherst’s For the Common Good: 2024–2034 Strategic Plan. This position is subject to final budgetary and administrative approval. Working Conditions Work is performed in a standard office or indoor university environment and involves minimal physical exertion. Work Schedule and Work Arrangement Anticipated start date of September 1, 2027. Monday - Friday, 40 hours a week. Salary Information The University expects to pay within an approximate range between $79,350 and $119,014 for this position. Please note: The low end of this range is based on the minimum salary for the rank included in the collective bargaining agreement between the University and the Massachusetts Society of Professors, UMass Amherst/MTA/NEA. The high-end of the pay range reflects an increase above the median salaries at this rank within the department for faculty who may have multiple years of faculty experience. The specific pay for this position will be determined by the University based on consideration of all relevant factors, including faculty rank and required relevant certifications, when and if it decides to extend an offer of employment. Special Instructions for Applicants Applicants must submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, research and teaching statements, along with the contact details of three references. They are also encouraged to include a list of courses they are interested in teaching. Search questions can be addressed to: Ho-Sung Kim, Search Chair, kimhs@umass.edu. Applicants should apply by the priority deadline of December 1, 2026, to ensure consideration. This position will remain open for the time period required by any applicable collective bargaining agreement and will continue until a suitable candidate pool is identified. Interested applicants are strongly encouraged to apply early. Apply Now! Sign up for job alerts! Job Alert dd63fc9d Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Departments College of Natural Sciences College of Natural Sciences Categories Academic Year (9 Months) College of Natural Sciences Full Time MSP On Site Tenure Track Academic Year (9 Months) College of Natural Sciences Full Time MSP On Site Tenure Track First Name (required) c2d41b22 Last Name (required) 2bfcfeba Email (required) ebc60567 Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Similar Jobs Assistant Professor of Scenic Design Humanities & Fine Arts The University of Massachusetts Amherst Department of Theater seeks a full-time tenure-track Assistant Professor of Scenic Design, with the position slated to begin September 1, 2027. The successful candidate will be an artist and teacher whose primary specialty is scenic design, and as a multi-disciplinary artist, has experience and expertise in some other area of theater (for example, other design areas, directing, puppetry, etc.). Reflecting the needs and advances of the industry, a candidate with experience in projection design or other technologies is desirable. Dining Hall Supervisor (Catering) Auxiliary Enterprises The Dining Hall Supervisor for Catering supervises and participates in the set-up of dining rooms and serving areas; assists in the serving of food and beverage; delivers and services coffee breaks and meals; supervises personnel assigned to the function; assists with maintaining records; trains and supervises student employees. Lecturer/Visiting Assistant Professor College of Natural Sciences The Department of Mathematics and Statistics (www.umass.edu/mathematics-statistics) at the University of Massachusetts Amherst invites applications for Visiting Assistant Professor positions (non-tenure track). One or more appointments may be a Marshall H. Stone Visiting Assistant Professorship, with a higher salary and an enhanced research fund. Candidates should have completed the Ph.D. by the beginning of the appointment. The initial appointment is for one (1) year and is ordinarily renewable for two (2) additional years. Exceptional promise in research and a commitment to outstanding teaching at all levels of the curriculum are expected. Ready to Apply? First Name (required) 8646d74b Last Name (required) 2329d32f Email (required) d6c76dc3 Refer someone to this job Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Candidate Details We use cookies. Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all Please choose the category/categories"
