    "https://careers.umass.edu/jobs/assistant-professor-tt-civil-environmental-engineering-structural-engineering-amherst-massachusetts-united-states": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (TT) - Civil & Environmental Engineering - Structural Engineering",
      "description": "Assistant Professor (TT) - Civil & Environmental Engineering - Structural Engineering - Amherst, Massachusetts, United States Assistant Professor (TT) - Civil & Environmental Engineering - Structural Engineering - Amherst, Massachusetts, United States Kiosk mode Assistant Professor (TT) - Civil & Environmental Engineering - Structural Engineering 510070 Amherst, Massachusetts, United States Academic Year (9 Months) Full Time MSP On Site Research & Laboratory Riccio College of Engineering Show More Show Less Faculty Opening on: Oct 16 2026 Closing on: Jan 15 2027 Title: Assistant Professor (TT) - Civil & Environmental Engineering - Structural Engineering Executive Area: Academic Affairs College/School/MBU: Engineering Department: Civil & Environ Engineering Work Location: Amherst Schedule: Full time Work Arrangement: Onsite Job Summary The Department of Civil & Environmental Engineering at the University of Massachusetts Amherst is seeking talented applicants qualified for tenure-track assistant professor position. Under exceptional circumstances, highly qualified candidates at other ranks may receive consideration. The appointment is expected to begin September 1, 2027. The Department is committed to building a vibrant and inclusive academic community that reflects and advances the university’s strategic priorities. The position is defined broadly across the entire scope of structural engineering and mechanics. Interdisciplinary scholars and those working in emerging areas are particularly encouraged. Specific areas of interest include but are not limited to: Sustainable, resilient and adaptive infrastructure systems; applications of AI, big data and machine learning to civil systems; Risk, reliability and hazard analysis and modeling; Large-scale testing; Advanced and architected materials and additive manufacturing; Innovative and unconventional structures, including energy infrastructure. As a campus, college, and department with an increasingly diverse student body, we are looking for candidates who can operate effectively in a diverse faculty and student community, and who value excellence, collegiality, and collaboration. We encourage applications from individuals with a commitment to advance the studies and development of all students seeking to become engineers. We welcome candidates who have had non-traditional career paths, those who have paused their careers for family or other reasons, or who spent time outside of academia. Essential Functions The candidate is expected to develop an externally-funded research program in civil engineering, structural engineering or engineering mechanics, and develop and maintain an internationally recognized scholarly presence. The candidate is expected to contribute to teaching existing graduate and undergraduate courses in civil engineering and mechanics, and will be encouraged to develop creative new curricula, including courses supporting the department's structural engineering and mechanics academic program. The candidate is also expected to contribute meaningful service to their department, College, University and externally within their field through participation in committees, faculty governance, and activities that support the academic community. Other Functions Other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Applicants must have a Ph.D. in Civil Engineering or a closely related field, or expect to complete such degree by September 1, 2027. Additional Details The Department of Civil & Environmental Engineering has 30 full-time, tenure track faculty members, more than 100 graduate students and approximately 400 undergraduates. The department’s FY 25 research expenditures exceeded $16 million. Additional information about the Department can be obtained at umass.edu/engineering/cee including information about the Brack and Boyle Structural Testing Laboratories. The Daniel J. Riccio Jr. College of Engineering is recognized as one of the top 35 public engineering schools in the nation by U.S. News & World Report and is the #1 public engineering program in New England. Our inclusive and dynamic community includes ~2700 undergraduate, ~650 graduate students and ~160 instructional faculty (full-time tenure stream and lecturers) across five engineering departments—biomedical, chemical and biomolecular, civil and environmental, electrical and computer, and mechanical and industrial. Our faculty members are developing life-saving medical devices and therapeutics; revolutionizing next-generation manufacturing and materials; advancing AI, robotics and autonomous systems; designing smart infrastructures; tackling climate change; and leading breakthroughs in computing and cybersecurity. They are recipients of prestigious national recognitions, including 22 CAREER Awards since 2020. With over $69 million in research expenditures and 20+ affiliated research centers and programs, we're at the forefront of discovery. Our faculty and students engage actively in innovation, transforming ideas into meaningful impact through entrepreneurship activities, patenting new discoveries, and new venture creation. Our new Sustainable Engineering Laboratories building offers immersive labs and learning spaces, enabling us to test and develop technologies that address real-world sustainability challenges. The College strives to be a leader in transformative engineering education, supporting faculty in the development of their teaching expertise through various initiatives such as our membership in the Kern Entrepreneurial Engineering Network (KEEN). For more on the Riccio College of Engineering and our mission, visit: https://www.umass.edu/engineering/ and https://www.umass.edu/engineering/about/mission-vision-and-inclusivity-statement ). This position is subject to final budgetary and administrative approval. Working Conditions Work is performed in a standard office or indoor university environment and involves minimal physical exertion. Work Schedule and Work Arrangement Full-Time, academic year position. Schedule varies depending on teaching, research and service. Salary Information The University expects to pay within an approximate range between $79,350 and $122,455 for this position. Please note: The low-end of the pay range in all faculty searches at the University of Massachusetts Amherst is the minimum salary for the rank included in the collective bargaining agreement between the University and the Massachusetts Society of Professors, UMass Amherst/MTA/NEA. Engineering TT starting salaries are typically well above this minimum. The high-end of the pay range reflects the 75th percentile for the department/discipline based on established, disciplinary-specific faculty compensation surveys. The specific pay for this position will be determined by the University based on consideration of all relevant factors, including faculty rank and required or relevant certifications, when and if it decides to extend an offer of employment. Special Instructions for Applicants Candidates must provide the following in their application package addressed to “CEE/SEM Search Committee.” Applications will be reviewed starting on December 1st, 2026 and reviewed until the search is completed. All files must be provided in .pdf format. Applicants must disclose in their cover letter whether and to what extent AI tools were used in the preparation of their application package. A cover letter, addressed to “CEE/SEM Search Committee”. The cover letter should a) introduce yourself, b) briefly describe your interest in and alignment with this position and the mission and vision of the Riccio College of Engineering. Page limit: 1 page An overall vision statement. Candidates should describe the overall vision of what they would like to accomplish in their career, integrating across all aspects of teaching, research, outreach, and service, and discuss the inspiration that will sustain them in this faculty position. Page Limit: 2 pages A research statement. Candidates should situate their past research in a broader scholarly context and give their research vision for this position. Candidates are specifically asked to mention the types of scholarship they will perform, how that scholarship will be disseminated, expectations for how that scholarship will be funded, collaboration potential, and any connections to community engagement, broadening participation in STEM, or grand challenges in engineering. Page limit: 2 pages A teaching and mentoring statement. Candidates should address teaching and mentoring of graduate and undergraduate students, as well as recruiting of graduate students. Candidates should describe their past teaching and mentoring experience and express their vision for educating the next generation of engineers. Candidates are specifically asked to mention existing courses in CEE/SEM they are qualified to offer and also mention courses they are interested in developing. Please discuss any plans for making engineering engaging to all students. Page limit: 2 pages A current curriculum vitae. The CV is typically ordered as follows: 1) education, 2) employment, 3) awards and honors, 4) sponsored research (if any), 5) peer reviewed publications, 6) service, and 7) other sections as considered appropriate by the candidate. Contact information (name, title, email address, institution) for 3 professional references. At least one reference should be from a current or former supervisor. Process The steps of the process are as follows: A minimally qualified pool is identified from the applicants, ideally by the end of December 2026. All remaining candidates are evaluated and a “long list” is identified, which will be interviewed via video conference. Candidates invited to these interviews will be emailed at least one week before the scheduled date. A “shortlist” is developed from the long list, and these candidates will be invited to interview on campus. While we anticipate interviews on campus in February 2027. For candidates advancing to the Short List stage: references will be contacted. This position will remain open for the time period required by any applicable collective bargaining agreement and will continue until a suitable candidate pool is identified. Interested applicants are strongly encouraged to apply early. Apply Now! Sign up for job alerts! Job Alert 95bdc1f6 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Departments Engineering Engineering Categories Academic Year (9 Months) Full Time MSP On Site Research & Laboratory Riccio College of Engineering Academic Year (9 Months) Full Time MSP On Site Research & Laboratory Riccio College of Engineering First Name (required) 0786d576 Last Name (required) d938a653 Email (required) 0b76df02 Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Similar Jobs Departmental Assistant - Patient Benefit and Referral Specialist Direct Medical Care Accurately performs all functions related to the patient intake process at University Health Services (UHS) by interviewing patients, verifying eligibility electronically via multi-payer systems and entering or updating information in the practice manager in a timely manner and with a high degree of accuracy. Ensures the facility will be reimbursed for the services performed by verifying eligibility and benefits and obtaining pre-determinations and authorizations. Assures timely access to care for patients. Collects co-pays and fee-for-service payments and prepares electronic reconciliation of daily log. Processes telephone traffic and urgent situations in a timely manner, supports and assists various departments, and utilizes the computer system to locate patients and providers. Verifies that all patients are complying with the UHS Eligibility policy. Post Doctoral Research Associate - Stephanie Delzell College of Natural Sciences Post Doc Research Associate will perform, research pertaining to barriers to molecule accumulation in bacterial pathogens in Siegrist lab. Assistant Professor (TT) - Civil & Environmental Engineering - Structural Engineering Engineering The Department of Civil & Environmental Engineering at the University of Massachusetts Amherst is seeking talented applicants qualified for tenure-track assistant professor position. Under exceptional circumstances, highly qualified candidates at other ranks may receive consideration. The appointment is expected to begin September 1, 2027. The Department is committed to building a vibrant and inclusive academic community that reflects and advances the university’s strategic priorities. Ready to Apply? First Name (required) 34404e85 Last Name (required) 350150f0 Email (required) cdb82767 Refer someone to this job Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Candidate Details We use cookies. Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all Please choose the category/categories"
