    "https://careers.umass.edu/jobs/lecturer-ecology-evolution-amherst-massachusetts-united-states": {
      "title": "Lecturer - Ecology & Evolution",
      "description": "Lecturer - Ecology & Evolution - Amherst, Massachusetts, United States Lecturer - Ecology & Evolution - Amherst, Massachusetts, United States Kiosk mode Lecturer - Ecology & Evolution 510001 Amherst, Massachusetts, United States Academic Year (9 Months) College of Natural Sciences Full Time Lecture & Non Tenure-Track MSP On Site Show More Show Less Faculty Opening on: Oct 21 2026 Add to favorites View favorites Title: Lecturer - Ecology & Evolution Executive Area: Academic Affairs College/School/MBU: College of Natural Sciences Department: Biology Work Location: Amherst Schedule: Full Time Work Arrangement: Onsite Job Summary The Department of Biology at the University of Massachusetts Amherst invites applicants for a full-time Continuing-Track (non-tenure track) Lecturer to teach introductory biology, ecology, and evolution courses for life-science and biology undergraduate students. The successful candidate will teach a large-enrollment introductory biology course, a 200-level ecology course, and a 200-level evolution course. This is an academic year, benefited, 2-year position and is renewable contingent upon satisfactory performance. Lecturers at the University of Massachusetts are unionized with the Massachusetts Society of Professors (MSP) and have the potential for long-term careers with advancement within the Lecturer ranks; this includes Continuing Appointment status after four years of service and Promotions after six years of service. The Department of Biology has a robust community of Lecturers who are dedicated to the ongoing improvement of the life science curriculum. Both the University and College of Natural Sciences invest in our faculty’s continued professional growth through the Center of Teaching and Learning, the Office of Faculty Development, and College of Natural Sciences Faculty Development Programs. Lecturers are also eligible to apply for numerous professional development grants and fellowships through the University and College of Natural Sciences. Essential Functions Teaching large lecture sections (100-200+ students) of Introductory Biology II (BIOL 152), Evolution (BIOL 280), and Ecology (BIOL 287). Implementing evidence-based pedagogical practices that promote active learning and student engagement. Developing experiential learning opportunities for students within the Biology major. Departmental and Institutional Service as appropriate for the career stage. Contributing to the Biology curriculum by participating in ongoing discussions of pedagogical best practices and science education research. Other Functions Work collaboratively and effectively to promote teamwork, equality and inclusiveness. Work in partnership with colleagues within the CNS community and across the campus to support the Dean’s strategic priorities. Perform other duties as assigned in support of the mission and goals of the College of Natural Sciences. Minimum Qualifications A Ph.D. in Biology or a closely-related field is required, and postdoctoral experience is preferred. Post-secondary teaching experience involving substantial responsibility for course instruction and/or course design is required, with a preference for experience teaching large-enrollment courses. Ability to implement evidence-based pedagogical practices that promote active learning and student engagement. Additional Details At UMass Amherst and in CNS, we strive to be a community where every individual feels a sense of belonging, where every individual is included, and where every individual is valued. These values and goals are reflected in the College of Natural Sciences’ Inclusive Excellence framework and in the UMass Amherst’s For the Common Good: 2024–2034 Strategic Plan. We invite candidates to describe how their work aligns with this university’s values and strategic goals in both their cover letter and statement of teaching philosophy and interests. This position is subject to final budgetary and administrative approval. Working Conditions Work is performed in a standard office or indoor university environment and involves minimal physical exertion. Work Schedule and Work Arrangement Anticipated start date of September 2027. Monday - Friday, 40 hours a week. Salary Information The University expects to pay within an approximate range between $65,550.00 and $87,601.00 for this position. Please note: The low end of this range is based on the minimum salary for the rank included in the collective bargaining agreement between the University and the Massachusetts Society of Professors, UMass Amherst/MTA/NEA. The high-end of the pay range reflects an increase above the median salaries at this rank within the department for faculty who may have multiple years of faculty experience. The specific pay for this position will be determined by the University based on consideration of all relevant factors, including faculty rank and required relevant certifications, when and if it decides to extend an offer of employment. Special Instructions for Applicants Along with the application, please submit a (1) cover letter, (2) curriculum vita, (3) a statement of teaching philosophy and interests, and (4) contact information for three professional references. Review of complete applications will begin on January 4, 2027 and will continue until the position is filled. If you have questions about this search, please contact the search committee chair, Caralyn Zehnder (czehnder@umass.edu). This position will remain open for the time period required by any applicable collective bargaining agreement and will continue until a suitable candidate pool is identified. Interested applicants are strongly encouraged to apply early. Apply Now! Sign up for job alerts! Job Alert de3c01bd Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Departments College of Natural Sciences College of Natural Sciences Categories Academic Year (9 Months) College of Natural Sciences Full Time Lecture & Non Tenure-Track MSP On Site Academic Year (9 Months) College of Natural Sciences Full Time Lecture & Non Tenure-Track MSP On Site First Name (required) f5c152f2 Last Name (required) 00703e40 Email (required) 95dae74c Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Similar Jobs Nutrition Aide (UMass Extension EFNEP Nutrition Education Aide, Springfield Office) College of Natural Sciences The EFNEP Nutrition Educator delivers public education for UMass Extension Nutrition Education Program to improve healthy food and lifestyle choices among at-risk, limited resource families and individuals encompassing all age groups in various locations throughout the Western Region of Massachusetts with the main office location located in Springfield, MA. This includes presenting a series of 8 to 10 lessons that focus on general nutrition, food preparation, food safety and food budgeting using approved curriculum materials in a variety of community settings. Recruitment of participants is also included as part of the workday. The individual will collect and manage participant data regarding knowledge, skills, attitudes, and behaviors; ensuring confidentiality at all times. Research Fellow - Fissore Lab College of Natural Sciences The Fissore Reproductive Physiology Lab is looking for a Research Fellow Lab Technician. We seek a meticulous, organized, and reliable Research Fellow committed to generating consistent and reproducible data. We will provide mentorship to carry out independent research projects, master laboratory techniques, develop and optimize new methods, and contribute to peer-reviewed publications. Post Doctoral Research Associate - Conservation Scientist College of Natural Sciences We are seeking applications for a Postdoctoral Researcher at the University of Massachusetts, Amherst to lead this work. The position blends human dimensions and quantitative wildlife disease research: analyzing existing statewide stakeholder surveys to understand knowledge, attitudes, and regulation-relevant behaviors; helping design and facilitate an interagency tabletop exercise that stress-tests the state's reactive response; and co-designing the communication materials and decision tools that come out of it. Depending on the candidate's interests and skills, there are also opportunities to contribute to other quantitative work on wildlife disease arrival and spread (e.g., evaluating which management actions perform best under alternative modes of arrival). Ready to Apply? First Name (required) 136d37dd Last Name (required) cd8d8a7a Email (required) 7605bf84 Refer someone to this job Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Candidate Details We use cookies. Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all Please choose the category/categories"
    },
    "https://careers.umass.edu/jobs/lecturer-director-of-design-build-program-amherst-massachusetts-united-states": {
      "title": "Lecturer - Director of Design Build Program",
      "description": "Lecturer - Director of Design Build Program - Amherst, Massachusetts, United States Lecturer - Director of Design Build Program - Amherst, Massachusetts, United States Kiosk mode Lecturer - Director of Design Build Program 510094 Amherst, Massachusetts, United States Calendar Year (12 Months) College of Natural Sciences Full Time Lecture & Non Tenure-Track MSP On Site Show More Show Less Faculty Opening on: Oct 21 2026 Add to favorites View favorites Title: Lecturer - Director of Design Build Program Executive Area: Academic Affairs College/School/MBU: College of Natural Sciences Department: Environmental Conservation Work Location: Amherst Schedule: Full Time Work Arrangement: Onsite Job Summary The Building and Construction Technology Program (in the Department of Environmental Conservation) and the Department of Architecture at the University of Massachusetts Amherst invites applications for a full-time lecturer position to be the director and primary instructor for the UMass DesignBuild program. The successful candidate will provide the core organizational, administrative, and instructional role in the UMass DesignBuild program. This program is committed to providing an experiential learning environment where students engage in design-build pedagogy and experience the integration of both architectural design and full-scale construction. Past projects featured high-performance building design, experimental materials and systems, and MEP/HVAC systems integration. Successful candidates will have a strong interest in design-build pedagogy and experience in architectural design, particularly for high-performance residential projects, and full-scale on-site construction work. Essential Functions Teaching Duties: Fall: Teaching the BCT 313 course (3-credit) on high-performance, light-frame, residential construction. This may coincide with instructional duties for the completion of the previous DesignBuild project. Spring: Teaching a 6-credit undergraduate and graduate level DesignBuild design studio (ARCH 404/604) that leads to the construction documents for the upcoming project. Summer: Instructor for two 6-credit (each) DesignBuild build courses during which the structure will be built. These are two full-time (40 hours/week) six-week sessions. Teaching duties may vary, depending on evolving needs. Service Duties Construction Management Duties: Perform all necessary tasks required for the successful construction of the proposed design-build project as specified in the UMass DesignBuild Design Studio, including but not limited to the following: Prepare UMass DesignBuild site in preparation for student arrival for the start of the Build, i.e., tools, materials, construction equipment, safety equipment, etc. Procure all materials and equipment necessary for the build. Instruct students in all aspects of construction procedures including both tool and job site safety. Provide technical on-site instruction about all systems (structural, MEP, etc.). Communicate and coordinate as necessary with assorted code officials, municipal authorities, specialty contractors, and materials suppliers. Complete construction as needed following the conclusion of the summer build courses and ensure delivery to community partner. Operating construction equipment and UMass vehicle(s). Program Management Duties (Throughout the Year): Primary management of the UMass Design Build program including, but not limited to the following tasks: Solicitation and procurement of projects for the DesignBuild program. Negotiations for agreements with community partners, the University, and/or other potential collaborators for DesignBuild projects. Collaborate with the Development Offices to secure philanthropic support for projects through cash and in-kind contributions. Pursue and coordinate project financing and fundraising strategies, including charitable gifts, grant funding, and the purchase or transfer of completed projects. Overseeing program and construction finances and interfacing with University accountants. Interfacing with UMass Legal as necessary for annual agreements with recipients of DesignBuild projects, and managing agreements between all involved parties. Student recruitment. Outreach to media, donors, town officials, AEC professionals, faculty, UMass Administers, etc. Campus construction site supervision (maintenance and updating). Other Functions Work collaboratively and effectively to promote teamwork, equality and inclusiveness. Work in partnership with colleagues within the CNS community and across the campus to support the Dean’s strategic priorities. Perform other duties as assigned in support of the mission and goals of the College of Natural Sciences. Minimum Qualifications Master’s degree in a relevant field (e.g. architecture, building science, construction technology, construction management, civil engineering, or similar). Construction Supervisor License (CSL) in Massachusetts or equivalent (by the time of hire). OSHA 10 Safety License (by the time of hire). Previous onsite experience in full-building light-frame construction. Previous teaching experience in construction, design-build, and/or design programs. Driver’s license. Preferred Qualifications Architect license in Massachusetts (by the time of hire). Experience in high-performance building design and construction. OSHA 30 safety license. Additional Details This position is a calendar year Lecturer (100% appointment, 12 months), joint hire between the Department of Environmental Conservation (ECO) as primary department and the Department of Architecture as secondary. The initial appointment will be for two years but is renewable based on performance and program need. The successful candidate will be a core faculty member of the Building and Construction Technology (BCT) Program in the Department of Environmental Conservation and the Department of Architecture and will interface with other academic groups at UMass and industry throughout the region. The ECO and Architecture departments and UMass Amherst place special emphasis on faculty-student interactions and a commitment to teaching and mentoring. We value the retention of new faculty, and the successful candidate will be provided with a departmental mentoring committee and have access to extensive university programs and mutual mentoring opportunities through the Colleges of Natural Sciences, Humanities and Fine Arts, and the University’s Office of Faculty Development. At UMass Amherst and in CNS, we strive to be a community where every individual feels a sense of belonging, where every individual is included, and where every individual is valued. These values and goals are reflected in the College of Natural Sciences’ Inclusive Excellence framework and in the UMass Amherst’s For the Common Good: 2024–2034 Strategic Plan. This position is subject to final budgetary and administrative approval. Working Conditions Work is performed in indoor and/or outdoor environments, and requires frequent lifting, bending, tool use or equipment use. May involve work in confined spaces or at heights. Work Schedule and Work Arrangement Monday - Friday, 40 hours per week The latest start date is September 1, 2027, but an early start in May or June 2027 is possible (to allow for observation of the current year’s build project). Salary Information The University expects to pay within an approximate range between $75,382 and $108,786 for this position. Please note: The low end of this range is based on the minimum salary for the rank included in the collective bargaining agreement between the University and the Massachusetts Society of Professors, UMass Amherst/MTA/NEA. The high-end of the pay range reflects an increase above the median salaries at this rank within the department for faculty who may have multiple years of faculty experience. The specific pay for this position will be determined by the University based on consideration of all relevant factors, including faculty rank and required relevant certifications, when and if it decides to extend an offer of employment. Special Instructions for Applicants Along with the application, please submit the following: Cover letter of no more than 1,000 words that describes your interest in the position, qualifications and teaching philosophy, Comprehensive CV, Documentation of required licenses, as applicable, Portfolio of design work or construction project listing, not to exceed 25MB, Contact information of three references (including name, title, address, telephone number, and email address). Application review will begin January 15, 2027 . This position will remain open for the time period required by any applicable collective bargaining agreement and will continue until a suitable candidate pool is identified. Interested applicants are strongly encouraged to apply early. Apply Now! Sign up for job alerts! Job Alert 583533db Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Departments College of Natural Sciences College of Natural Sciences Categories Calendar Year (12 Months) College of Natural Sciences Full Time Lecture & Non Tenure-Track MSP On Site Calendar Year (12 Months) College of Natural Sciences Full Time Lecture & Non Tenure-Track MSP On Site First Name (required) 21625121 Last Name (required) b340b4ec Email (required) 716b03d8 Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Similar Jobs Nutrition Aide (UMass Extension EFNEP Nutrition Education Aide, Springfield Office) College of Natural Sciences The EFNEP Nutrition Educator delivers public education for UMass Extension Nutrition Education Program to improve healthy food and lifestyle choices among at-risk, limited resource families and individuals encompassing all age groups in various locations throughout the Western Region of Massachusetts with the main office location located in Springfield, MA. This includes presenting a series of 8 to 10 lessons that focus on general nutrition, food preparation, food safety and food budgeting using approved curriculum materials in a variety of community settings. Recruitment of participants is also included as part of the workday. The individual will collect and manage participant data regarding knowledge, skills, attitudes, and behaviors; ensuring confidentiality at all times. Research Fellow - Fissore Lab College of Natural Sciences The Fissore Reproductive Physiology Lab is looking for a Research Fellow Lab Technician. We seek a meticulous, organized, and reliable Research Fellow committed to generating consistent and reproducible data. We will provide mentorship to carry out independent research projects, master laboratory techniques, develop and optimize new methods, and contribute to peer-reviewed publications. Post Doctoral Research Associate - Conservation Scientist College of Natural Sciences We are seeking applications for a Postdoctoral Researcher at the University of Massachusetts, Amherst to lead this work. The position blends human dimensions and quantitative wildlife disease research: analyzing existing statewide stakeholder surveys to understand knowledge, attitudes, and regulation-relevant behaviors; helping design and facilitate an interagency tabletop exercise that stress-tests the state's reactive response; and co-designing the communication materials and decision tools that come out of it. Depending on the candidate's interests and skills, there are also opportunities to contribute to other quantitative work on wildlife disease arrival and spread (e.g., evaluating which management actions perform best under alternative modes of arrival). Ready to Apply? First Name (required) 271d0f17 Last Name (required) 27145f2e Email (required) b1831f38 Refer someone to this job Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Candidate 